course: dictionary
lesson: 5996
title: Measles: a virus that spreads
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Measles is an illness caused by a virus. A virus is a tiny germ that gets inside your body and makes you ill. Measles spreads very easily from person to person.
explain: The signs are a high fever, a cough, red eyes and a red, blotchy rash all over the body. Someone with measles must stay at home so they do not pass it on. People say they «have measles» or «catch measles».
explain: Context helps you understand the word. «The MMR vaccine protects against measles, mumps and rubella in two doses in early childhood.» The vaccine protects, so a jab can stop measles, and the sentence tells you the jab is given when children are very young.
fact: Measles is caused by a virus, which is a tiny germ.
fact: «Measles is one of the most infectious diseases known», which means it spreads very easily.
fact: Measles can cause pneumonia and brain swelling, so it can be serious, even dangerous, for young children.
fact: The MMR vaccine protects against measles, mumps and rubella.
fact: Herd immunity happens when nearly everyone in a group is protected, so a germ cannot find enough people to jump to, and babies too young for the jab stay safe.
exercise: «Measles is one of the most infectious diseases known.» What does infectious mean? | Lasts for only one day | Impossible to see with the eyes | *Easily passed from one person to another
exercise: What is measles caused by? | A cold wind | *A virus, a tiny germ | A broken bone | Eating too many sweets
exercise: Why do people with measles stay at home? | *So they do not spread it to other people | Because schools are closed for the holidays | Because the rash is a lovely colour
test: «The MMR vaccine protects against measles, mumps and rubella.» What does protects mean here? | Costs a lot of money | Tastes sweet | *Keeps you safe from catching an illness | Makes you ill on purpose
test: Why can a germ like measles struggle to spread in a class where nearly everyone is protected? | Because the children are all outside | Because measles only spreads at night | Because the germ is too big to move | *Because most people are already protected, so there are few people left to catch it
exam: Which best explains herd immunity, from «when nearly everyone in a class is immune»? | When a herd of cows visits the school | When every child in a class has a cold | When the vaccine is given to animals only | *When most people are protected, so a germ struggles to spread and babies too young for the jab stay safe
